%description:

RFC 9293 section 3.8.6.2.1 — a window update that opens a full segment ends the hold of the sender SWS avoidance at once.

The rule: "Send data: (1) if a maximum-sized segment can be sent". While the override timer of rule (4) runs, a
window update of the peer that opens room for a full segment lets the held data go at once; the sender does not wait
for the override timeout. Silly window avoidance is out of the scope of the catalog of RFC 9293
(doc/project/evidence/standard/rfc9293/catalog.md, "Out of scope in this catalog"), so this test checks the section
directly. The reviewer document is ../sws-override-timer.md.

Host under test: host1, with an MSS of 1000 bytes and an RTO of 1 s, sends 5000 bytes. The TS option takes 12 bytes of
each segment, so a full segment holds 988 bytes. Peer: host2, with an advertised window of 3464 bytes, reads nothing
until 0.5 s. After three segments, 2964 bytes, the window of host2 is 500 bytes, and host1 holds the rest. At 0.5 s
host2 reads all. With windowUpdateAfterRead, the read sends the window update at once: the default sends the new
window only with the next ACK, and host2 has nothing to acknowledge.

Observation: host1 sends no data while it holds, and sends data right after the window update, long before the
override timeout. This test keeps the behavior of a window update while the repair of BD-21 of
plan/pending/baseline-catch-up-defects.md lets the override timer run through segments that update no window.

Expected result: PASS.

%includes:
#include "ProtocolTest.h"
#include "TcpTestApp.h"

%global:

using namespace inet;
using namespace inet::protocoltest;

Define_ProtocolTestProgram()
{
    return ProtocolTest("Rfc9293SwsOverrideWindowUpdate")
        .act(action().at(0.0).run<TcpTestApp>("host2.app[0]", [](TcpTestApp& app) {
            app.socket().setAutoRead(false);
            app.socket().bind(1000);
            app.socket().listen();
        }))
        .once(on("host2.tcp").signal("packetSentToLower")
             .filterPacket("tcp.ackBit == true && tcp.window == 500")
             .describe("the ACK of host2 that leaves a window of 500 bytes: host1 holds its data")
             .within(0.45))
        .never(on("host1.tcp").signal("packetSentToLower")
              .filterPacket("tcp.synBit == false && pk.dataLength > tcp.headerLength")
              .describe("held data before the window update")
              .within(0.29))
        .act(action().at(0.5).run<TcpTestApp>("host2.app[0]", [](TcpTestApp& app) {
            app.accepted(0).read(65535);
        }))
        .once(on("host2.tcp").signal("packetSentToLower")
             .filterPacket("tcp.ackBit == true && tcp.window >= 1000")
             .describe("the window update of host2 after its read")
             .within(0.2))
        .once(on("host1.tcp").signal("packetSentToLower")
             .filterPacket("tcp.synBit == false && pk.dataLength > tcp.headerLength")
             .describe("the held data, right after the window update and before the override timeout")
             .within(0.1));
}

%activity:
EV << "Rfc9293SwsOverrideWindowUpdate compiled\n";

%inifile: omnetpp.ini

[General]
**.tcp.mss = 1000
*.host2.tcp.advertisedWindow = 3464
*.host2.tcp.windowUpdateAfterRead = true
*.host1.app[0].sendBytes = 5000B
*.host2.app[0].typename = "TcpTestApp"
include ../../parameter/ini/_bulk.ini
include ../../parameter/ini/_base.ini

%contains: stdout
PROTOCOLTEST Rfc9293SwsOverrideWindowUpdate: PASS
